{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>But this is the house of Elohim, and this is the gate of the heavens.</b> It is the Temple, which is a gate for prayers and sacrifices to ascend from there to the heavens. Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 28:17): Rabbi Elazar said in the name of Rabbi Yosei ben Zimra (Genesis Rabbah 69:7): This ladder stands in Be'er Sheva, and its slope reaches to the Temple — Be'er Sheva stands in the south of Judah and Jerusalem in the north, at the border between Judah and Benjamin; and Bethel was in the north of the territory of Benjamin, at the border between Benjamin and the children of Joseph. Thus the ladder, its feet in Be'er Sheva and its head in Bethel, has its slope reaching opposite Jerusalem. And our Rabbis said (Chulin 91a): \"This righteous man came to my inn\" — and they further said (Pesachim 88a): Jacob called it Bethel, and this is Luz, not Jerusalem. From where did they learn to say this? I say that Mount Moriah was uprooted and came here, and this is the contraction of the earth spoken of in Chulin (91a), that the Temple came to meet him as far as Bethel, and this is \"and he encountered the place.\" And if you say, when our father Jacob passed by the Temple, why did they not detain him there — he did not pay attention to pray in the place where his fathers had prayed, and from heaven they detained him; he proceeded to Haran, as we say in the chapter Gid HaNasheh (Chulin 91a). And Scripture supports us: \"and he went to Haran\" — for when he reached Haran he said: Perhaps I passed by the place where my fathers prayed and I did not pray there. He resolved to return, and went back as far as Bethel; the earth contracted for him as far as Bethel. All these are the words of the Rav [Maimonides], and it does not seem correct to me at all, for the contraction of the earth mentioned regarding Jacob is only like that which they said regarding Eliezer, servant of Abraham, who came to Haran in a single day, as they said in Sanhedrin (95a): The earth contracted for three: Eliezer servant of Abraham, Jacob our father, and Abishai son of Zeruiah. And they explained: Eliezer servant of Abraham — as it is written (Genesis 24:42), \"and I came this day to the spring,\" to say that on that very day he set out. Jacob — as it is written, \"and he encountered the place,\" for when he reached Haran he said: Perhaps I passed by the place where my fathers prayed and I did not pray there; once he resolved in his mind to return, the earth contracted for him, and immediately \"he encountered the place.\" Behold, in the explanation itself they say that since it arose in his heart in Haran to return, the earth contracted for him and he encountered the place where his fathers had prayed — not that he returned to Bethel, and not that Mount Moriah was uprooted and came there. And in Genesis Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 59:11) they further make both of them equal regarding the contraction: they said, \"And he arose and went to Aram Naharaim\" (Genesis 24:10) — in a single day; \"and I came this day to the spring\" — this day I set out and this day I arrived. And regarding Jacob they similarly expounded (Genesis Rabbah 68:8): \"and he went to Haran\" — the Rabbis say, in a single day. Now what reason would there be for Mount Moriah to be uprooted and come as far as Bethel, after Jacob took the trouble to return from Haran to Bethel — a journey of many days? Furthermore, Bethel is not the edge of the land of Israel on the side of Haran, for Haran is the land of the east. Moreover, the middle point is not called the slope of the ladder; and furthermore, what reason is there for it to be opposite Bethel, when the middle point indicates nothing more than the whole? But these midrashim have another meaning entirely. They said there (Genesis Rabbah 63:10): Rabbi Hoshaya said, it is already written (Genesis 28:7), \"And Jacob listened to his father and his mother and went to Paddan-aram\" — so what does Scripture teach by saying, \"And Jacob went out from Be'er Sheva\"? Rather, he said: My father — when he sought to go outside the land, from where was he given permission? From Be'er Sheva. So I too am going to Be'er Sheva: if He gives me permission, I go; and if not, I do not go. Therefore Scripture needed to say, \"And Jacob went out from Be'er Sheva.\" And the intent of this midrash is that they hold Jacob was blessed in Hebron, which is the land of his father's sojourning, and to there he returned to him, as it is said (Genesis 35:27), \"And Jacob came to Isaac his father at Mamre, Kiryat Arba, which is Hebron, where Abraham and Isaac sojourned.\" If so, the verse that says \"And Jacob went out from Be'er Sheva\" means that when his father commanded him to go to Laban, he went there [to Be'er Sheva] to receive permission, and it is the place where he lodged and saw visions of Elohim, and [HaShem] gave him permission to leave, as He said: \"and I will keep you wherever you go, and I will bring you back to this soil.\" And the ladder that he saw, according to the opinion of Rabbi Yosei ben Zimra, he saw that its feet were in Be'er Sheva, in the place where he was lying there, and the end of its slope — which is the head of the ladder — reaches opposite the Temple, resting in the heavens at the opening through which the angels enter and exit, and the glorious Name was standing upon it. Therefore he knew that Be'er Sheva is the gate of the heavens, good for prayer, and the Temple is the house of Elohim. And in the morning Jacob set out from Be'er Sheva and went to Haran in a single day, and that is the contraction of his journey. This is the opinion of Rabbi Yosei ben Zimra, who said in Genesis Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 69:7): This ladder stands in Be'er Sheva and its slope reaches to the Temple, as it is said: \"And Jacob went out from Be'er Sheva, and he feared, and said: How awesome is this place.\" And the stone that he set as a monument — he did not erect it in the place where he slept, for Be'er Sheva is not Bethel. Rather, he erected it in Bethel, and to there he returned on his way back, as it is said (Genesis 35:1): \"Arise, go up to Bethel, and make there an altar to the El who appeared to you,\" etc. But he erected it opposite the slope, in the place where the head of the ladder stands, which he called \"house of Elohim,\" and that is the city of Luz. And according to the opinion of Rabbi Yosei ben Zimra, Luz is Jerusalem, which he called Bethel. And this is possible according to the verses in the book of Joshua. And in truth, it is not Bethel near Ai, for that was the name of that city from the beginning, in the days of Abraham and earlier. Rabbi Yehuda bar Rabbi Simon disagreed there (Genesis Rabbah 69:7) with Rabbi Yosei ben Zimra, and said: This ladder stands in the Temple and its slope reaches to Bethel — what is his reason? \"And he feared and said: How awesome is this place,\" etc., \"and he called the name of that place Bethel.\" And the opinion of Rabbi Yehuda bar Rabbi Simon is that \"and he encountered the place\" is Mount Moriah; \"and he lodged there because the sun had set\" — it set for him out of its time, as they said: \"This righteous man came to my inn\" and was dismissed without lodging. And he saw the ladder, its feet in that place and its slope — which is its head — reaching opposite Bethel, which is the city of Luz. And he said that this place is the house of Elohim, and the slope of the ladder is the gate of the heavens. Thus Mount Moriah is good for prayer, and Bethel too is a place fit to serve Elohim there. And he erected the monument in Bethel, for according to all opinions he erected it opposite the slope. And the opinion of Rabbi Yehuda bar Rabbi Simon agrees with the midrash in the Gemara in the chapter Gid HaNasheh (Chulin 91a) and in the chapter Chelek (Sanhedrin 95a), that Jacob went out from Be'er Sheva and traveled to Haran, and when he decided to return and pray in the place where his fathers had prayed, on Mount Moriah, then the earth contracted for him and he immediately encountered Mount Moriah. And perhaps their opinion is that it contracted for him both on the way there and on the way back, according to the words of the one who says (Genesis Rabbah 68:8): \"and he went to Haran\" — in a single day; \"and he encountered the place\" — immediately, in a sudden instant. And I further found explicitly in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer HaGadol (chapter 35): He said: Jacob was seventy-seven years old when he went out from his father's house. And the well [of Be'er Sheva] traveled before him from Be'er Sheva to Mount Moriah, a journey of two days, and he arrived there at midday, etc. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Jacob, the bread is in your pack and the well is before you — eat and drink and lie down in this place. He said before Him: Master of all worlds, until now the sun still has fifty rungs of descent, and yet I am lying down in this place. And out of its time the sun came from the west, and Jacob looked and saw the sun setting in the west: \"and he lodged there because the sun had set.\" Jacob took twelve stones from the stones of the altar upon which his father Isaac was bound, and placed them at his head, to inform him that twelve tribes were destined to arise from him, and they all became one stone, to inform him that they were all destined to become one nation in the land, as it is said (II Samuel 7:23): \"and who is like Your people Israel, one nation.\" Jacob rose early in the morning with great fear, and said: The house of the Holy One, blessed be He, is in this place, as it is said: \"and he feared and said: How awesome is this place.\" From here you learn that whoever prays in Jerusalem is as though he prays before the Throne of Glory, for the gate of the heavens is open to hear the prayer of Israel, as it is said: \"and this is the gate of the heavens.\" And Jacob returned to gather the stones and found them all to be one stone, and placed it as a monument in the midst of the place, and oil descended for him from the heavens and he poured it upon it, as it is said: \"and he poured oil upon its head.\" What did the Holy One, blessed be He, do? He took His right foot and sank the stone into the depths of the abysses, and made it a supporting peg for the earth, like a person who places a supporting peg under a turban. Therefore it is called Even Shetiyah (the Foundation Stone), for there is the navel of the earth, and from there the earth was spread out, and upon it the sanctuary of YHWH stands, as it is said: \"and this stone, which I have set as a monument, shall be a house of Elohim.\" And from there he set out, and in the blink of an eye he came to Haran. End quote. And behold, all these midrashim, even though there are differences among them in some details, all agree that the contraction of the earth happened for Jacob, that he traveled a journey of many days in the blink of an eye. And it is possible that they all agree with one another, and in all his journeys the earth contracted for him: when he went from Be'er Sheva to Haran, and when he desired to return to Mount Moriah, and when he departed from there to Haran. And not one of all the midrashim says as Rashi does:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "Ramban here attributes to 'the Rav' (Maimonides) the view that Mount Moriah was miraculously uprooted to Bethel, but the subsequent arguments and sources cited are drawn from Rashi's commentary and Talmudic/aggadic passages; the attribution may refer to a different medieval authority or to Rashi himself as 'the Rav' in this context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:28.019119499Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}